{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.StandardModel.TypeBFoldingChiralityObstruction","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.StandardModel.TypeBFoldingChiralityObstruction","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:QOU.StandardModel.ChiralityGradingConstruction"],"declarations":4,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.StandardModel.Chirality.folding_inducing_gen1_chirality_is_free","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.StandardModel.Chirality.folding_inducing_gen1_chirality_is_free","kind":"theorem","line":117,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/StandardModel/TypeBFoldingChiralityObstruction.lean#L117-L133","doc":"**Instantiated at the corpus's own sector.**\n\n`flipChirality` on `Gen1Quark` is free (V10, `flipChirality_free`, by\ninspection of the eight states).  So *any* folding involution that induces it\nis free as well — it has no fixed point at all, hence no stable matter in the\nsense the folding conjecture assigns to fixed points.\n\nThis is the sharp form of the finding: the separation is not hypothetical.\nThe corpus already carries a chirality involution with a *proved* fixed-point\nstructure, and it is the opposite of the folding conjecture's. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.StandardModel.Chirality.global_chirality_forces_no_fixed_matter","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.StandardModel.Chirality.global_chirality_forces_no_fixed_matter","kind":"theorem","line":107,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/StandardModel/TypeBFoldingChiralityObstruction.lean#L107-L115","doc":"**Contrapositive, as the usable form.**  A global grading forces the\ninvolution free, i.e. forbids stable matter in the folding conjecture's\nfixed-point sense. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.StandardModel.Chirality.no_state_chirality_of_folding_fixed_point","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.StandardModel.Chirality.no_state_chirality_of_folding_fixed_point","kind":"theorem","line":88,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/StandardModel/TypeBFoldingChiralityObstruction.lean#L88-L105","doc":"**The obstruction transfers along an intertwiner.**\n\n`π` maps the folded crystal to the physical state space and intertwines the\nfolding involution `foldι` with the state involution `stateι` — this is\nprecisely what \"the folding *induces* the chirality involution\" means.  Then a\nsingle fixed point of `foldι` (one piece of stable matter) rules out any\nglobally odd grading downstairs.\n\nNote `π` need be neither injective nor surjective: the obstruction is carried\nby one point, so no structure on `π` beyond equivariance is required. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.StandardModel.Chirality.stable_matter_and_global_chirality_incompatible","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.StandardModel.Chirality.stable_matter_and_global_chirality_incompatible","kind":"theorem","line":76,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/StandardModel/TypeBFoldingChiralityObstruction.lean#L76-L86","doc":"**The dilemma.**  One involution cannot both fix a state and admit a\nglobally odd grading.\n\nRead against the folding conjecture: \"stable matter = fixed points\" and \"there\nis a chirality grading odd under this involution\" are inconsistent as claims\nabout a single `ι`. ","signatureChunk":0}]}